<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Yaxha</h1><p class="st-meta">400 CE – 800 CE (approx.) · capital Yaxha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Classic Maya city on the shore of Lake Yaxha in north-eastern Petén, a long-time rival of nearby Naranjo.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Yaxha, one of the few Maya cities whose ancient name survives, grew on a ridge above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-yaxha">Lake Yaxha</a> from the Preclassic period. By the Early Classic it was a substantial city with its own emblem glyph and plazas laid out in Tikal-related styles.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city had more than 500 structures, including acropolis complexes, twin-pyramid groups like those at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tikal">Tikal</a>, and causeways leading to the lakeshore. Its rulers fought frequently with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naranjo">Naranjo</a>: in 710 Naranjo's king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahk-tiliw-chan-chaak">K'ahk' Tiliw Chan Chaak</a> claimed a victory over Yaxha and the scattering of a former Yaxha king's bones, and Naranjo attacked again in 799.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Yaxha declined around 800 CE and was abandoned soon after, though smaller settlements persisted on lake islands into the Postclassic. It is now part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaxha-nakum-naranjo-national-park">Yaxha-Nakum-Naranjo National Park</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yaxha.jpg" alt="Yaxha" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Marie Carianna · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YaxhaNorthAcropolis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 CE – 800 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yaxha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classic Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Guatemala</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yaxa'</dd></dl></aside></div>

